WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:03.500
Film Courage: What were some of the biggest
mistakes you made in the beginning of your

00:00:03.500 --> 00:00:06.370
screenplay writing and how did you realize
they were mistakes?

00:00:06.370 --> 00:00:11.700
Aleem Hossain, filmmaker and AR/VR creator,
assistant professor in media arts and culture

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:12.700
at Occidental College: I think…that’s
a good question.

00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:13.700
What are the mistakes?

00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:15.300
I made all sorts of mistakes when I was starting
to write

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:18.150
But I don’t necessarily think of them as
mistakes and more I think it’s just the

00:00:18.150 --> 00:00:25.310
process of learning and I’m happy that I
was able to at the time just keep writing

00:00:25.310 --> 00:00:26.310
and writing.

00:00:26.310 --> 00:00:30.060
Things that jump to mind when I think of my
particular mistakes.

00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:36.850
I think one I was a little scared of trusting
my own point of view.

00:00:36.850 --> 00:00:41.970
I think I was relying too much on the models
of screenwriting looking at books on The Hero’s

00:00:41.970 --> 00:00:45.780
Journey and three-act structure and others.

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:47.200
I was just obsessed with those things.

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:49.120
I was trying to find an answer in them.

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:55.020
At the end of the day I do think they have
some use but for me I realized that when I

00:00:55.020 --> 00:00:59.270
just let go of that and asked myself just
core emotional and dramatic questions…first

00:00:59.270 --> 00:01:02.130
of all actually sometimes it would result
in stuff that actually had that structure

00:01:02.130 --> 00:01:06.299
anyway inherent in it and even when it didn’t
it still worked so definitely I think sort

00:01:06.299 --> 00:01:07.810
of avoiding the structure.

00:01:07.810 --> 00:01:14.670
I think honestly also not outlining and spending
enough time planning.

00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:18.229
There is such a temptation to feel like you’re
not getting anything done until you type on

00:01:18.229 --> 00:01:19.229
the page.

00:01:19.229 --> 00:01:22.569
I feel like anything what I’ve learned in
every time I write a screenplay I think now

00:01:22.569 --> 00:01:28.210
I spend longer in the brainstorming and then
outlining phase each time realizing that it’s

00:01:28.210 --> 00:01:30.150
so easy to think “I just typed a scene!

00:01:30.150 --> 00:01:31.150
That means I made progress!”

00:01:31.150 --> 00:01:35.600
But if it’s not the right scene, not the
right entry to that scene, not the right character,

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:39.740
not the right time, that time doesn’t really
get me anywhere.

00:01:39.740 --> 00:01:42.329
So I’m really trying to like…and it’s
hard to do that.

00:01:42.329 --> 00:01:43.729
It’s hard to stay in the brainstorming phase.

00:01:43.729 --> 00:01:46.529
It can feel like “Oh my gosh I’m not making
progress.”

00:01:46.529 --> 00:01:50.250
Quite literally we use page count as our metric
of progress.

00:01:50.250 --> 00:01:52.420
I wrote five pages today.

00:01:52.420 --> 00:01:57.520
And I do think that in some ways it is destructive
to creativity like sitting…there are times

00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:02.201
where I have sat for an hour staring out the
window and had no good thoughts until the

00:02:02.201 --> 00:02:08.190
final two minutes but that one idea was so
good and shaped the whole film, that hour,

00:02:08.190 --> 00:02:10.760
the 58 minutes where I didn’t think anything
was worth that two minutes, those two minutes

00:02:10.760 --> 00:02:14.890
might be worth weeks of writing just to have
that right brainstorm.

00:02:14.890 --> 00:02:19.082
So for sure I think I rushed too quickly into
writing pages was another mistake I made.

00:02:19.082 --> 00:02:23.970
And then lastly, I really do believe (it’s
related to outlining) I don’t think you

00:02:23.970 --> 00:02:25.790
should start writing the movie until you know
the end.

00:02:25.790 --> 00:02:30.140
I think the end of a movie is so important
because it should inform everything that comes

00:02:30.140 --> 00:02:31.140
before it.

00:02:31.140 --> 00:02:35.320
I think it in some ways no matter whether
you’re making a super clear movie or some

00:02:35.320 --> 00:02:40.060
ambiguous movie, whatever the ending is (in
the classic set up of a joke) if that’s

00:02:40.060 --> 00:02:42.520
the punchline, the set up has to be right
for it.

00:02:42.520 --> 00:02:47.040
And I think writing any pages before you know
where that’s coming is a mistake even if

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:50.270
your ending is open-ended I think you need
to know you’re leading to that.

00:02:50.270 --> 00:02:51.640
And so until I know the end I won’t stop
writing.

